\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

How many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We translate variance components into item budgets via D-study projections.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold using single-model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) MMLU variance components. The multi-facet design (1,296 conditions per item) achieves $G = 0.936$ at 200 items and requires only 55 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design reaches only $G = 0.592$ at 200 items and requires 552 items---a 10.0$\times$ budget ratio stable across subset sizes (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs (single-model, MMLU). Total evals = items $\times$ conditions.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{lcrrrc}
\toprule
Design & Cond. & $n_{G{\geq}.80}$ & Total & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 1{,}296 & \textbf{55} & 71{,}280 & \textbf{.936} & --- \\
Single-cond. & 1 & 552 & 552 & .592 & .457 \\
IRT+multi & 1{,}296 & --- & 150{,}336 & --- & .895$^\ddagger$ \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & --- & 116 & 116 & --- & .457$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT 116 items \cite{zhou2026irt} under single-condition: $G = 0.457$. $^\ddagger$\,Same 116 items under multi-facet: $G = 0.895$.}
\end{table}

\citet{zhou2026irt}'s IRT-selected 116 items yield $G = 0.457$ under single-condition but $G = 0.895$ under multi-facet conditions, demonstrating that IRT item selection and G-theory protocol optimization are orthogonal and complementary. An internal consistency check using four-model subsets confirms projection stability ($|\Delta G| \leq 0.029$ for MC, $\leq 0.065$ for FF; Table~\ref{tab:forward_pred}).

For practitioners, the design choice depends on the evaluation bottleneck: the full 1,296-condition design minimizes items (55 items, 71,280 evaluations per model); the recommended 3-prompt $\times$ 3-seed design balances cost and reliability (178 items, 1,602 evaluations); single-condition designs require 552 items at minimal per-item cost. Multi-facet designs suit settings where item construction is expensive (e.g., expert-crafted benchmarks); more items under fewer conditions suit compute-constrained evaluation. A full adoption gradient is in Appendix~\ref{app:intermediate_dstudy}. Cross-model projections show budget ratios of 11--22$\times$ across benchmarks (bootstrap CIs all exceed 10$\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:dstudy_cross}), with multi-facet budgets ranging from 71 to 162 items (Table~\ref{tab:dstudy_cross}). Figure~\ref{fig:teaser} visualizes the D-study curves.

\paragraph{Projection assumptions.} Cross-model budgets increase to 93 items because model$\times$item interaction enters relative error variance. Bootstrap CIs (71--162 items across benchmarks) capture item-level variability; model-level stability is assessed via LOMO (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}). All projections assume the 2024--2025 model generation; the 72B extension (\S4.6) provides preliminary out-of-sample support.

\subsection{Preliminary Scale Validation}

Within-family analysis (Qwen2.5 3B/7B/72B on MMLU) shows qualitatively invariant variance structure: item difficulty dominates at all three scales (73--83\%, all $G > 0.95$) with consistent facet ranking. The proportional increase at 72B is consistent with the Bernoulli variance ceiling: total variance (0.113) matches $p(1{-}p)$ at grand mean 0.87, compressing all components while item difficulty---the hardest to eliminate---retains the largest share. A crossed 3B$\times$7B$\times$72B design yields model$\times$item = 38.5\% $>$ item = 35.7\%, reflecting expected DIF amplification across a 68--87\% accuracy range ($G_\text{item} = 0.720$; three models insufficient). The out-of-sample 72B provides preliminary within-family evidence that variance structure is stable across a 24$\times$ parameter range within Qwen2.5; cross-family generalization remains untested.

\paragraph{Robustness checks.} Three MMLU resamples yield CV = 3.1\%; treating models as fixed increases $G$ by 0.077--0.113, confirming conservative budgets (\S\ref{sec:model_facet}; seven additional analyses in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).

